{{documentation}}
Helps create the json to generate charts using [https://www.chartjs.org/ Chart.js] through [[MediaWiki:Gadget-Charts-core.js]].

{{Helper module|name = Chart data
|fname1 = _main(&nbsp;args&nbsp;)
|ftype1 = table/chart
|fuse1 = Turns a table/chart object into a json string.

|fname2 = <nowiki>convertToXYFormat(&nbsp;ys, [xs|{}]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype2 = table, table
|fuse2 = Converts the <code>ys</code> array into an array of <code>{x = n, y = y[n]}</code> tables. If <code>xs</code> is already partially filled it will use <code>{x = x[n], y = y[n]}</code> until all values in <code>xs</code> are used, then it will use <code>{x = n, y = y[n]}</code> again for the remaining values in <code>ys</code>.

|fname3 = <nowiki>generateXYFromFunc(&nbsp;func, start_x, end_x, [step|1]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype3 = function, number, number, number
|fuse3 = Returns an array of <code>{x = n, y = fun(n)}</code> tables where n ranges from <code>start_x</code> to <code>end_x</code> in <code>step</code> increments. Be careful when using decimal <code>step</code> values as floating point error can cause the generator to stop one element too soon.

|fname4 = <nowiki>jagexInterpolation(&nbsp;low_chance, high_chance, start_level, end_level&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype4 = number, number, number, number
|fuse4 =

|fname5 = <nowiki>newChart(&nbsp;[options]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype5 = table
|fuse5 = Returns a new chart object. <code>options</code> is a table with options in the [https://www.chartjs.org/ Chart.js] format. Most options will be set automatically or will be set later with other functions if not already defined. Usually all you need to define here is the chart type e.g. <code>newChart{ type = 'scatter' }</code>. '''Check the modules documentation for more info'''.

|fname6 = <nowiki>chart:addDataSets(&nbsp;...&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype6 = table/dataSet object
|fuse6 = Appends all given data sets to the <code>chart.data.datasets</code> table.

|fname7 = <nowiki>chart:addDataLabels(&nbsp;labels&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype7 = table
|fuse7 = Appends all items in <code>labels</code> to the <code>chart.data.labels</code> table.

|fname8 = <nowiki>chart:setDimensions(&nbsp;width, [height|width], [minWidth|400], [minHeight|400], [resizable|false]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype8 = number/string, number/string, number/string, number/string, boolean
|fuse8 = Sets the dimensions of the chart. If a number is given to <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, <code>minWidth</code> and <code>minHeight</code> it will be assumed you meant pixels. You can also use strings like <code>100%</code> to fill the available space, <code>40vw</code>/<code>40vh</code> to dynamically scale the simensions to the viewport size (i.e. 40vw = 40% of browser's window width). If <code>resizable</code> is true, the chart can be dragged by the lower right corner to change its size.

|fname9 = <nowiki>chart:setTitle(&nbsp;[text|nil], [position|'top']&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype9 = string, string
|fuse9 = Sets the label title of the chart. A value of nil will remove the current title.

|fname10 = <nowiki>chart:setXLabel(&nbsp;[label|nil]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype10 = string
|fuse10 = Sets the label for the x axis. Only works on chart types 'line', 'bar', 'horizontalBar', 'bubble' and 'scatter'. If used without arguments it will remove the current label.

|fname11 = <nowiki>chart:setYLabel(&nbsp;[label|nil]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype11 = string
|fuse11 = Sets the label for the y axis. Only works on chart types 'line', 'bar', 'horizontalBar', 'bubble' and 'scatter'. If used without arguments it will remove the current label.

|fname12 = <nowiki>chart:setXLimits(&nbsp;[min|nil], [max|nil], [step|nil]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype12 = number, number, number
|fuse12 = Sets the start, stop and step size of the x axis. Any argument with a value of nil will remove that setting. Only works on chart types 'horizontalBar', 'bubble' and 'scatter'.

|fname13 = <nowiki>chart:setYLimits(&nbsp;[min|nil], [max|nil], [step|nil]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype13 = number, number, number
|fuse13 = Sets the start, stop and step size of the y axis. Any argument with a value of nil will remove that setting. Only works on chart types 'line', 'bar', 'bubble' and 'scatter'.

|fname14 = <nowiki>chart:setRadialLimits(&nbsp;[min|nil], [max|nil], [step|nil]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype14 = number, number, number
|fuse14 = Sets the start, stop and step size of the r axis on polar plots. Any argument with a value of nil will remove that setting. Only works on chart types 'radar' and 'polarArea'.

|fname15 = <nowiki>chart:setXAxisType(&nbsp;[type|nil]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype15 = string
|fuse15 = Sets the axis type. Supported values are 'linear', 'logarithmic', 'category' and 'time'. If called without arguments it will reset back to the default value 'linear'. Only works on chart types 'bubble', 'scatter' and 'horizontalBar'.

|fname16 = <nowiki>chart:setYAxisType(&nbsp;[type|nil]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype16 = string
|fuse16 = Same as <code>chart:setXAxisType()</code> but for the y axis. Only works on chart types 'line', 'bubble', 'scatter' and 'bar'.

|fname17 = <nowiki>chart:setOptions(&nbsp;options&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype17 = table
|fuse17 = Sets options using [https://www.chartjs.org/ Chart.js] format but makes sure you only change the given settings and not accidentally delete already existing settings. i.e. using <code><nowiki>chart:setOptions{ options = {scales = {ticks = {max = 100}}} }</nowiki></code> while <code><nowiki>{options = {scales = {ticks = {min = 0}}}}</nowiki></code> already exists will result in <code><nowiki>{options = {scales = {ticks = {min = 0, max = 100}}}}</nowiki></code>.

|fname18 = <nowiki>chart:makeMwLoadDataCompatible()</nowiki>
|ftype18 = N/A
|fuse18 = Strips metatables so that it can be loaded by <code>mw.loadData()</code>making it possible to display the table using {{t|Chart data|<module name>}}. This should only be used at the very end when you are done creating your chart.

|fname19 = <nowiki>chart:newDataSet(&nbsp;[options]&nbsp;)</nowiki>
|ftype19 = table
|fuse19 = Returns a new dataSet object which is also automatically added to the chart datasets table.

|fname20 = dataSet:addData(&nbsp;data&nbsp;)
|ftype20 = table
|fuse20 = Appends the values of <code>data</code> to the already existing data stored in the dataSet.data array. Using <code>dataset.data = data</code> will overwrite any stored data.

|fname21 = dataSet:addDataPoint(&nbsp;data&nbsp;)
|ftype21 = number/table
|fuse21 = Append a single value to the <code>dataSet.data</code> array. Same as <code>table.insert(&nbsp;dataSet.data, data&nbsp;)</code>.

|fname22 = dataSet:setOptions(&nbsp;options&nbsp;)
|ftype22 = table
|fuse22 = Sets options using [https://www.chartjs.org/ Chart.js] format.

|fname23 = chart:setTooltipFormat(&nbsp;format&nbsp;)
|ftype23 = string
|fuse23 = Set the tooltip format to either 'skillingSuccess', where y values are displayed as percents, 'harvestLives', where y values are displayed as-is, or 'custom'. For custom tooltips, a property called 'label' must be included with each dataPoint, for example <code>dataSet:addDataPoint({x=99, y=13034431, label="XP at level 99"})</code>.

|example =
<syntaxhighlight lang='lua'>
local p = {}
local chart = require( 'Module:Chart data' )

-- This chart can then be added to a page using {{Chart data|<module name>}}
function p.pie()
    local plot = chart.newChart{ type = 'pie' }
    plot:setDimensions( '40vw', nil, 400, nil, true ) -- Pie chart is always square
    plot.colorPallet = chart.colorPallets.green
    local labels = {}
    local set = plot:newDataSet()

    for i = 1, 6 do
        set:addDataPoint( math.floor( math.sqrt( i ) * 10 + 0.5 ) / 10 )
        table.insert( labels, 'Value ' .. i )
    end

    plot:addDataLabels( labels )

    return plot:makeMwLoadDataCompatible()
end

return p.pie()

-------------------------------------------------------------
local p = {}
local chart = require( 'Module:Chart data' )

-- This chart cound be used by another module or drawn using {{#Invoke:<module name>|bar}}
function p.bar()
    local plot = chart.newChart{ type = 'bar' }
    plot:setDimensions( '40%', 600, 400, 400, true )
    plot:setXLabel( 'x axis label' )
    plot:setYLabel( 'y axis label' )

    for i = 1, 2 do
        local set = plot:newDataSet()
        for j = 1, 6 do
            set:addDataPoint( math.sqrt( i*j ) )
        end
        set.label = 'Set ' .. i

        if i == 1 then
            set.color = chart.colorPallets.green[3]
        else
            set.color = chart.colorPallets.orange[3]
        end
    end

    local labels = {}

    for i = 1, 6 do
        table.insert( labels, 'Value ' .. i )
    end

    plot:addDataLabels( labels )

    return plot
end

return p
</syntaxhighlight>
